# v4.0 - Changelog | Resources - Gel Docs
created: undefined, last updated: 2026-03-12, [Read more](https://docs.geldata.com/resources/changelog/4_x)

type Item {
required available: bool {
default := false;
};
required name: str;
required description: str;
index fts::index on (
fts::with_options(
.name,
language := fts::Language.eng
)
);
}
```
The `fts::index` indicates to EdgeDB that this object type is a valid target
for full-text search.
The property that will be searched as well as the
language is provided in the index.
The `fts::search()` (added in 4.0) function allows searching objects for a particular phrase:
...
select fts::search(Item, 'candy corn', language := 'eng');
...
The search results are provided as a tuple containing the matching document
object and a score.
Higher score indicates a better match.
So we can use these
values to order the results:
...
```
with res := (
select fts::search(Item, 'candy corn', language := 'eng')
)
select res.object {name, score := res.score}
order by res.score desc;
```

# Schema | Learn
created: undefined, last updated: 2026-07-23, [Read more](https://docs.geldata.com/learn/schema)

Use the `required` and `multi` keywords to specify the cardinality of the
relation.
Copy
```
type Movie {
required title: str;
cinematographer: Person; # zero or one
required director: Person; # exactly one
multi writers: Person; # zero or more
required multi actors: Person; # one or more
}
type Person {
...
}
```
...
```
type Movie {
required title: str;
multi actors: Person;
# returns all movies with same title
multi same_title := (
with t := .title
select detached Movie filter .title = t
)
}
```

# Link properties - Schema | Reference
created: undefined, last updated: 2026-06-25, [Read more](https://docs.geldata.com/reference/datamodel/linkprops)

Links, like objects, can also contain **properties**.
These are used to store metadata about the link.
Due to how they're persisted under the hood, link properties have the additional constraint of always being `single`.
...
In practice, link properties are best used with many-to-many relationships
(`multi` links without any exclusive constraints).
For one-to-one,
one-to-many, and many-to-one relationships the same data should be stored in
object properties instead.
...
type Person {
required name: str { constraint exclusive };
multi friends: Person {
strength: float64;
}
}
...
required name: str { constraint exclusive };
multi friends: Person {
strength: float64;
constraint expression on (
__subject__@strength >= 0
...
A way to conceptualize the difference between a regular property and
a link property is that regular properties are used to construct an object,
while link properties are used to construct the link between objects.
...
To select a link property, you can use the `@<>name` syntax inside the
select *shape*.
...
required name: str;
multi follows: Person {
followed: datetime {
default := datetime_of_statement();
};
};

# GitHub - geldata/gel: Gel supercharges Postgres with a modern data model, graph queries, Auth & AI solutions, and much more.
created: 2017-06-29, last updated: 2025-07-16, [Read more](http://github.com/geldata/gel)

Instead, Gel thinks about schema the same way you do: as **object types** containing **properties** connected by **links**.
```
type Person {
required name: str;
}
type Movie {
required title: str;
multi actors: Person;
}
```

# What is EdgeDB's prefer schema of representing interaction data? · geldata gel · Discussion #2088
created: 2020-12-27, last updated: 2025-05-21, [Read more](https://github.com/geldata/gel/discussions/2088)

You don't need to manually create relation ids, links take care of that for you.
Here's how I'd design your schema:
```
type person {
required property name -> str;
multi link partners -> person;
};
```
If you have relationship-specific data you can use link properties for that:
```
type person {
required property name -> str;
multi link partners -> person {
property sent -> int64;
}
};
```
and then query it like
```
select person {
name,
partners: person {
name,
@sent
}
}
```

